<section id="intelligence-needs-a-place-to-show-its-work">
<span class="ls-essay-section-number">04</span>
<h2>Keep the right to question the result</h2>
<p>The appeal of capable AI can make verification feel like an interruption. Yet the person whose name is on the work still needs to understand what they are accepting. A plausible paragraph can take longer to verify than it took to generate. A beautiful chart can conceal a poor comparison.</p>
<p>In <em>Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability</em>, researchers evaluated 15 models across two benchmarks. They found only small improvements in reliability alongside recent capability gains, and distinguished successful answers from consistent behavior, robustness, and predictable failure. Those results describe the evaluated models and tasks; they are no measurement of Flow. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.16666" rel="noreferrer" target="_blank">Rabanser and colleagues</a>
</p>
<p>Flow’s Overnight notes begin with a deliberately narrow assignment. The model receives the page’s own numbers. Every number in its draft is checked against a source row. If a number cannot be traced, Flow asks for a correction and withholds the notes if the problem remains.</p>
<p>This establishes that a number has a source in the supplied data. It cannot establish that the source is correct, the interpretation is fair, or the conclusion deserves publication. Your experience and judgment still matter at each of those steps.</p>
<p>Flow’s <a href="/flow/receipts/" rel="noreferrer" target="_blank">receipts and evidence</a> keep a record beside the work: the model and route, cost and its basis, checks and their results, and revisions before and after a change. An estimate must remain distinguishable from an observed charge. A previous version provides a way back.</p>
<p>A person can take a larger creative risk when a smaller operational mistake is easier to see and recover from. Visible evidence gives exploration a firmer footing.</p>
</section>
